  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nLight rain showers have developed over parts of the region early \nthis morning. These showers are driven by low pressure system that \nwill continue to slide east today leading to rain becoming more \nwidespread later by midday. With the center of the low sliding just \nto the south, colder air will fill in from north allowing rain to \nbecome a rain/snow mix and eventually just snow overnight. Timing of \nthe precipitation type transition will depend on how quickly the \ncolder air moves in. Rain will change over to snow in the higher \nelevations first but most valleys should see snow by Friday morning. \nWith surface temperatures stuck in the 30s, snow continues through \nmuch of the daytime Friday thanks to wraparound moisture on the back \nside of the low. As the low drifts out to sea later on Friday, \ncoverage will become more scattered on the back side of the system. \nMeanwhile, low-level flow will continue to advect colder air over \nthe Great Lakes, leading to lake effect snow precipitation \ndeveloping.  \n\nSnowfall totals did not change much from the previous forecast. \nHigher elevations of the Catskills still could see the highest \ntotals with localized amounts of 2 to 3 inches. Other higher \nelevations across the region could see an inch or more. A coating of \nsnow is possible elsewhere as snow sticks to colder surfaces but is \nnot expected to accumulate much on the warmer ground. If rain \ntransitions to snow quicker than expected, then final snowfall \namounts could end up higher. These forecasted totals are still short \nof advisory level and confidence is too low for any headline \nissuance at this time.\n\nTemperatures through the end of the work week will be colder than \npast days. Highs today will be in the 40s and 50s before falling to \nnear and below freezing tonight. Temperatures will struggle to warm \nup Friday, only maxing out in the 30s to low 40s. Then sub-freezing \ntemperatures are expected Friday night and this could lead to light \nicing on untreated, wet surfaces. NBM guidance seemed too cold at \nnight with cloud cover and precipitation present. Then for the same \nreasons, the NBM was too warm compared to most other guidance for \nFriday. A blend of Consall and NBM was used to make adjustments to \nthe temperature forecast during these times.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nFlow will become northwesterly and 850mb temperatures fall colder on \nSaturday, leading to lake effect precipitation lingering through at \nleast Sunday and potentially into Monday as well. Along the northern \nareas of the Finger Lakes, precipitation will be primarily rain or \nrain/snow mix due to warmer conditions. Elsewhere, precipitation \nwill be mainly snow, at least on Saturday. As temperatures trend \nwarmer Sunday into Monday, snow will transition back to rain during \nthe day. The CMC keeps the lake effect showers going through Monday \nthough drier air begins to move in as an upper level ridge \napproaches the region. Additional snowfall accumulations are \nexpected through at least Saturday night, though any transition back \nto rain will likely melt most of what does accumulation. Another 1 \nto 2 inches will be possible in higher elevations whereas less than \nan inch is expected elsewhere. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nAfter this brief sample of winter, weather conditions improve during \nthe midweek period. The aforementioned ridge builds into the region \nTuesday with high pressure present through at least Thursday. \nTemperatures will trend warm and back above average, at least for \ndaytime highs. The nights will remain cool with temperatures near or \nbelow freezing.",
